+# Bug Assessment: Category deletion removes products
+
+- **Slug**: ticket
+- **Created**: 2026-11-18T00:00:00Z
+- **Source**: pasted text
+- **Verdict**: valid
+- **Severity**: critical
+
+## Report (verbatim or summarized)
+
+A merchant deleting the “Summer sale” category loses products that were only assigned to it. Existing carts and orders containing those products then fail, and opening the deleted category by id returns a server error. DELETE `/api/v1/private/category/{id}` must remove only the category and its descendants, preserve products and their data, detach only the deleted categories, preserve cart/order behavior, return 404 for deleted or nonexistent categories, and make deleted categories absent from category and product listings.
+
+## Symptom
+
+Category deletion currently deletes products when they have no category outside the deletion subtree, causing product, cart, and order data loss. Category lookup/deletion also needs to consistently treat missing categories as not found rather than allowing an error path.
+
+## Reproduction
+
+1. Create a parent category and at least one child category, assign products to those categories, including a product with no category outside the subtree; optionally put products in a cart and order.
+2. DELETE `/api/v1/private/category/{id}` for the parent category.
+3. Verify the products, stock, price, cart, and order remain usable; verify the parent and descendants are absent from category/product-by-category results and GET `/api/v1/category/{id}` returns 404.
+4. DELETE a nonexistent category id and GET a deleted category id; both should return 404.
+
+## Suspected Code Paths
+
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/services/catalog/category/CategoryServiceImpl.java:231-284` — category deletion finds products in the subtree, removes category associations, but calls `productService.delete` for products with only those associations.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/facade/category/CategoryFacadeImpl.java:360-374` — delete facade resolves the category and should preserve the not-found path for an absent id.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/api/v1/category/CategoryApi.java:222-235` — DELETE endpoint delegates category deletion and exposes the facade exception mapping.
+- `sm-core-model/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/model/catalog/category/Category.java:64-70` — descendant categories are configured for cascade removal; product links are owned by `Product.categories`.
+- `sm-core-model/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/model/catalog/product/Product.java:94-116` — product/category join mapping is owned by the product, so associations must be removed and persisted before category rows are deleted.
+
+## Root Cause Hypothesis
+
+High confidence: `CategoryServiceImpl.delete` intentionally treats a product with no remaining category as disposable and invokes the full product deletion service. That service clears associations and deletes product-owned data, which explains the missing products and downstream cart/order failures. The fix is to remove the affected category associations from every affected product and persist the products, never delete them; then delete the category entity so its descendant cascade removes the category subtree. The existing facade lookup already throws `ResourceNotFoundException` for a missing category, but regression tests should cover the public 404 behavior.
+
+## Proposed Remediation
+
+**Preferred**: Change `CategoryServiceImpl.delete` so every product associated with the target category or any descendant has only those category associations removed, then is updated. Do not call `productService.delete` during category deletion. Keep the existing reverse-order/subtree deletion and category cascade so descendants and their descriptions are removed after join-table associations are detached. Retain the facade’s store-scoped lookup so a missing or cross-store category returns `ResourceNotFoundException` and HTTP 404.
+
+Add focused integration coverage for a parent/child category subtree and products that are exclusive to the subtree versus shared with another category. Assert products and their inventory/price remain, deleted categories are absent from category and category-filtered product results, GET and DELETE missing ids return 404, and existing cart/order flows remain usable where the test fixtures support them.
+
+**Files likely to change**:
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/services/catalog/category/CategoryServiceImpl.java`
+- `sm-shop/src/test/java/com/salesmanager/test/shop/integration/category/CategoryManagementAPIIntegrationTest.java`
+- `sm-core/src/test/java/com/salesmanager/test/catalog/CategoryTest.java` (if service-level coverage is more appropriate)
+
+**Tests to add or update**:
+- Verify deleting a category subtree preserves products assigned only to it and products shared with another category, while detaching all deleted category links.
+- Verify descendant categories are deleted and product/category listing no longer returns them.
+- Verify GET and DELETE for missing category ids return HTTP 404.
+- Verify product inventory/price and cart/order access are not affected by category deletion, using existing integration fixtures where possible.
+
+## Risks & Considerations
+
+- Product updates must occur before category rows are deleted because the product owns the many-to-many join table.
+- The operation may update many products and should remain transactional through the existing service transaction configuration.
+- Existing category cascade behavior must be retained so descendants are removed without deleting products.
+- Tests may require the application integration database configuration and can be slower than unit tests.
+
+## Open Questions
+
+- [NEEDS CLARIFICATION: None for implementation; end-to-end cart/order assertions depend on available integration fixtures.]
